Patch 06d: orientation partition pruning, un-blunted. Supersedes 06, 06b and
06c -- apply INSTEAD of any of them, never on top.

06c did cut BD-rate substantially, but reading the two classes together shows
it did so by the wrong mechanism:

    06   A1 +14.00% / +0.75%  ratio 18.7    A2 +18.52% / +1.29%  ratio 14.4
    06c  A1  +6.07% / +0.29%  ratio 20.9    A2  +6.21% / +0.46%  ratio 13.5

    A1: speed -57%, BD -61%   ratio 18.7 -> 20.9
    A2: speed -66%, BD -64%   ratio 14.4 -> 13.5  (WORSE)

Both classes lost speed and quality cost in near-equal proportion, which is
what a blunt reduction in pruning strength does. A ratio only moves when a
change removes prunes that are expensive in quality and cheap in speed. 06c
mostly just pruned less of everything, and on A2 it came out behind.

The decisive detail is that A1's block-size floor never changed -- 4K keeps a
floor of 16 in both 06 and 06c -- so A1's entire -57% speedup came from the
variance floor and the frame gate, not from the resolution scaling that was the
point of 06c.

Two changes, both aimed at restoring speed that was surrendered cheaply.

1. The frame gate is removed.

   Measured: it withheld pruning from frames low in the reference pyramid,
   which are exactly the frames given the deepest search and therefore the most
   expensive. It gave up speedup where speedup was most available.

   Structural, and the part I should have seen when writing 06b: this heuristic
   profiles the SOURCE block. Source structure drives the partition choice most
   directly when there is no prediction to flatten it, so the test is at its
   most trustworthy on intra frames -- the very frames the gate disabled it on.
   It was protecting the case the heuristic handles best.

2. The resolution floor is relaxed for extended partitions only.

   06c raised one floor for every partition type. But the patch already grades
   partitions by value: plain rectangular partitions demand anisotropy 8 before
   being dropped, extended ones only 4, because rectangular partitions carry far
   more of the coding gain. The block-size floor should grade the same way.

   So at 1080p the rectangular partitions keep the protective floor of 32 while
   extended partitions return to 16, restoring pruning on 16x16..31x31 blocks.
   Those prunes are the cheaper half in quality terms, and 06c discarded them
   together with the expensive ones. 4K is unaffected: both floors are 16 there.

The variance floor is unchanged and carried forward.

Expected direction: more speedup than 06c on both classes, and more BD-rate
too. The bet is that speed returns faster than quality cost does, because both
changes restore prunes that were cheap in quality -- extended partitions, and
intra frames where the source profile is most valid.

The risk is specific and worth stating: 06c PASSES on A1 at ratio 20.9, and
that margin is thin. If BD-rate returns faster than speed here, 06d could fall
below the bar on a class that currently clears it. 06c is measured and
known-good, so it should stay in the round as the fallback arm rather than
being replaced by 06d outright.

A2 remains the unsolved class. It has been roughly 30% worse than A1 in every
variant so far (18.7/14.4, 20.9/13.5), which is too consistent to be tuning.
The likeliest cause is the one named above: the profile is measured on the
source, while 1080p at 33 frames spends most of its blocks on inter prediction,
where the residual and not the source decides whether a cut pays. If 06d does
not move A2, the next step is a prediction-aware profile, not another
threshold.

